  pEn=[
   "For four hundred years the men who governed Wales from London treated the Welsh language as something to be got rid of. An Act of Parliament shut it out of the courts and out of public office. A government report called it a barrier to progress. Schools hung a block of wood round the necks of children who spoke it. And yet, today, Welsh is spoken in the homes, schools, courts and Senedd of Wales, and it is the official language of the land. This is the story of how they tried to wipe out our language, and how the people of Wales refused to let it go.",
   "|| 1536 | Henry VIII’s Act of Union bars anyone who uses Welsh from public office unless they use English",
   "|| 1847 | The Blue Books call Welsh ‘a vast drawback to Wales’",
   "|| About 1800 to 1900 | The Welsh Not is hung round children’s necks in schools",
   "|| 1870 | The Education Act brings an English-only school within reach of every child",
   "|| 1885 | Dan Isaac Davies and his friends found a society to bring Welsh into the schools",
   "|| 1922 | Ifan ab Owen Edwards founds Urdd Gobaith Cymru",
   "|| 1942 | The Welsh Courts Act strikes out Henry VIII’s English-only courts clause",
   "|| 1962 | Saunders Lewis broadcasts Tynged yr Iaith, and Cymdeithas yr Iaith is born",
   "|| 1967 | The Welsh Language Act gives Welsh ‘equal validity’",
   "|| 1982 | S4C goes on air, after Gwynfor Evans’s vow to fast",
   "|| 1993 | The Welsh Language Act: Welsh and English on a basis of equality, and Henry VIII’s Act repealed at last",
   "|| 2011 | The Welsh Language Measure: ‘The Welsh language has official status in Wales’",
   "## The first language of Britain",
   "Welsh was here first. It grew out of Brittonic, the language of the Britons, spoken across this island long before English came over the North Sea. Aneirin and Taliesin sang in it in the sixth century. When Hywel Dda gathered the laws of Wales at Whitland in the tenth century, the law of the land was set down in Welsh. The princes held their courts in it, the poets praised them in it, and the monks wrote the chronicles of the nation in it. For a thousand years it was the language of a whole people, from the prince’s hall to the shepherd’s hut.",
   "## 1536: ‘utterly to extirp’",
   "When Harri Tudur, a Welshman, took the crown at Bosworth in 1485, many in Wales hoped that their day had come. His son, Henry VIII, had other plans. In 1536 Parliament passed the Act that later generations called the Act of Union, which joined Wales to England in law. Its opening words did not hide what London thought of the Welsh tongue. The people of Wales, it said,",
   "> have and do daily use a speech nothing like nor consonant to the natural mother tongue used within this realm. | Laws in Wales Act (27 Henry VIII, c. 26), the preamble, as quoted by the History of Parliament Trust",
   "The King meant, the Act went on, ‘utterly to extirp all and singular the sinister usages and customs’ that set Wales apart. To extirp is to pull up by the roots. Then came the language clause. Every court in Wales was to be held in English, and:",
   "> No person or persons that use the Welsh speech or language shall have or enjoy any manner office or fees within this realm of England, Wales, or other the King’s dominion, upon pain of forfeiting the same offices or fees, unless he or they use and exercise the English speech or language. | Laws in Wales Act (27 Henry VIII, c. 26), the language clause",
   "At a stroke, the language of nine in every ten people in Wales was shut out of the courts and out of public office in its own land. A farmer from Llŷn or Ceredigion now stood in court while his case was argued in a tongue he did not understand. The gentry, who wanted posts and power, began to speak English and to send their sons to English schools. The poets, who had lived for centuries by the welcome of those great houses, slowly lost their patrons. The Act did not forbid anyone to speak Welsh at home. It did something more lasting: it made Welsh the language of the governed, and English the language of those who governed.",
   "## The Bible that saved the language",
   "In 1563 Parliament passed another Act, and its aim was much the same. It ordered the Bible and the Book of Common Prayer to be put into Welsh, so that the people of Wales could follow the new Protestant faith. But it also ordered an English Bible to be set beside the Welsh one in every church, so that by comparing the two the people would learn English the sooner.",
   "It did not turn out that way. William Salesbury gave Wales the New Testament in 1567, and William Morgan, vicar of Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant and later bishop, gave it the whole Bible in 1588. His Welsh was so fine that it set the standard for the written language for the next four hundred years. Every Sunday, in every parish in Wales, the people heard their own language in its noblest form. In the 1700s the circulating schools of Griffith Jones of Llanddowror taught perhaps half the people of Wales to read, in Welsh, from that Bible. A law meant to bring English in had given Welsh its greatest book.",
   "## 1847: the Treachery of the Blue Books",
   "By the 1840s Wales was changing fast. Iron and coal were drawing thousands into the valleys, the Chartists had marched on Newport, and the Daughters of Rebecca were pulling down the tollgates. In London the government wanted to know why the Welsh were so restless. In 1846 Parliament agreed to an inquiry into education in Wales, and three young English barristers were sent to carry it out: Ralph Lingen, Jelinger Symons and Henry Vaughan Johnson. Not one of them spoke Welsh. They went from school to school with interpreters, and much of their evidence came from Anglican clergymen in a country where most people worshipped in the chapels.",
   "Their reports came out in 1847, three thick volumes bound in blue. They found many poor schools, which was just as true of England. But they went much further. They called the Welsh dirty, idle and dishonest, they slandered the women of Wales, and they blamed the chapels and the language. Symons wrote:",
   "> The Welsh language is a vast drawback to Wales, and a manifold barrier to the moral progress and commercial prosperity of the people. It is not easy to over-estimate its evil effects. | J. C. Symons, report on Brecknock, Cardigan and Radnor, in Reports of the Commissioners of Inquiry into the State of Education in Wales (1847)",
   "Wales was furious, and Wales answered back. The minister and journalist Ieuan Gwynedd took the reports apart, fact by fact. Henry Richard, later known across Europe as the Apostle of Peace, spoke against them up and down the country. Lewis Edwards of Bala attacked them in the pages of Y Traethodydd. Jane Williams of Ysgafell wrote a whole book in reply, Artegall (1848). In 1854 the poet R. J. Derfel published a play that gave the reports the name they have carried ever since: Brad y Llyfrau Gleision, the Treachery of the Blue Books, an echo of the old tale of Brad y Cyllyll Hirion, the Treachery of the Long Knives. More than a century later the historian Kenneth O. Morgan called the reports ‘the Glencoe and the Amritsar of Welsh history’.",
   "The deepest harm was quieter. Many Welsh people came to believe what the Blue Books said: that to get on in the world, a child must leave Welsh behind.",
   "## The Welsh Not",
   "In the schools, that belief took the shape of a piece of wood.",
   "!! board",
   "The Welsh Not was a small board or stick, often cut with the letters W.N., and hung on a string. When the teacher heard a child speak Welsh, the board went round that child’s neck. The only way to be rid of it was to catch another child speaking Welsh and tell the teacher, and then the board passed on. Whoever was wearing it when school ended was punished, often with the cane. It went by many names: the Welsh Note, the Welsh stick, the Welsh lump, the Welsh mark, the cwstom. It is found in Welsh schools from the 1790s, and it was most common in the first half of the 1800s.",
   "Think what that did to a child. It turned friends into informers, and it taught every child that the language of their mother, their chapel and their own hearth was a thing to be ashamed of. Sir O. M. Edwards, born at Llanuwchllyn in 1858, wore the Welsh Not in his village school, and in his book of memories, Clych Atgof (1906), he told how it was hung round his own neck. He never forgot it.",
   "By 1879 the Welsh children’s magazine Trysorfa y Plant could look back on it as something from the past, but a past still raw in the memory of grown men:",
   "!! note",
   "> This phrase, ‘Welsh Note’, may be strange to all the children of Wales. We have not seen it, nor heard so much as its name, for many years. But forty or fifty years ago the children of the day schools of Wales knew well what the Welsh Note was. It poured more shame and tears than words can tell into the cup of crowds of men who are still alive and well. | Trysorfa y Plant, October 1879 (our translation)",
   "No Act of Parliament ever ordered the Welsh Not. Teachers chose to use it, and some parents even asked for it, because the state had made English the key to every good job and they wanted better for their children. But it grew out of a system run from London that treated Welsh as a mark of backwardness and gave it no place in the classroom. The historian Martin Johnes, in the first full study of the Welsh Not (2024), found that it did not even work: schools that shut Welsh out were poor at teaching English too. Welsh Nots still survive. You can see them at St Fagans National Museum of History, at Storiel in Bangor and in museums across Wales.",
   "## 1870: a school for every child, in English",
   "The Welsh Not was fading, but the state was about to reach further into Welsh childhood than ever before. Since 1862 government money for schools had been paid ‘by results’: each year an inspector tested the children, and the school’s grant depended on their marks in English reading, writing and sums. Then came the Elementary Education Act of 1870. It set up school boards to build schools wherever there were not enough, and from 1880 every child had to attend. For the first time almost every child in Wales sat in a classroom, and the classroom was English.",
   "The Act said nothing at all about Welsh. The lessons, the books and the tests were in English, in villages where the children might hear barely a word of English from one week to the next. When the census first asked about language, in 1891, about half the people of Wales spoke Welsh. Yet in the schools that were meant to serve them, their language was not on the timetable.",
   "## Wales fights back in the classroom",
   "The answer came from Wales itself. At the National Eisteddfod in Aberdare in 1885, Dan Isaac Davies and his friends founded the Society for the Utilisation of the Welsh Language, to bring Welsh into the schools. Davies dreamed of what he called Tair Miliwn o Gymry Dwyieithog, three million bilingual Welsh people. Within a year Welsh could earn a school a government grant, and the royal commission on elementary education took up his plans for teaching through both languages in its report of 1888.",
   "In 1907 the boy who had worn the Welsh Not, O. M. Edwards, became the first Chief Inspector of the new Welsh Department of the Board of Education. He worked to put the Welsh language and the history of Wales into the schools of Wales. His son, Ifan ab Owen Edwards, founded Urdd Gobaith Cymru in 1922, so that the children of Wales could play, camp and compete in their own language, and in 1939 he opened the first Welsh-medium school, in Aberystwyth. The first Welsh-medium school run by a local council followed at Llanelli in 1947, and the first Welsh-medium secondary school, Ysgol Glan Clwyd, in 1956. Today about a quarter of the children of Wales are taught through Welsh.",
   "## The courts: from Penyberth to 1942",
   "In 1936 Saunders Lewis, Lewis Valentine and D. J. Williams set fire to buildings at the RAF bombing school being built at Penyberth in Llŷn, and gave themselves up to the police. When the jury at Caernarfon could not agree, the retrial was taken away from Wales to the Old Bailey in London, and the three were jailed for nine months. To many in Wales it showed how little the law of the land thought of the language of the land.",
   "In 1938 a national petition, launched at the National Eisteddfod, gathered more than a quarter of a million names asking for Welsh to stand equal with English in law. The answer, the Welsh Courts Act of 1942, was small, but it did one great thing. It struck out the clause of Henry VIII’s Act that made English the only language of the courts, and it let anyone speak Welsh in court who would otherwise be at a disadvantage. After four hundred and six years, the first stone had been pulled out of the wall.",
   "## 1962: Tynged yr Iaith",
   "In Llangennech, near Llanelli, a miner, Trefor Beasley, and his wife Eileen, a teacher, refused from 1952 to pay their rates until the council sent them a demand in Welsh. They were taken to court again and again, and the bailiffs carried off their furniture, their piano and even food from their pantry. In 1960 the council gave way and sent a demand in Welsh, and the Beasleys paid at once.",
   "On 13 February 1962 Saunders Lewis gave the BBC’s annual radio lecture in Wales. He called it Tynged yr Iaith, the Fate of the Language. He warned that if things went on as they were, Welsh would end as a living language at about the beginning of the twenty-first century. He praised the Beasleys, and he called on the people of Wales to follow them: to make it impossible for the business of local and central government to carry on without Welsh.",
   "> Nid dim llai na chwyldroad yw adfer yr iaith Gymraeg yng Nghymru. Trwy ddulliau chwyldro yn unig y mae llwyddo. (To restore the Welsh language in Wales is nothing less than a revolution. Only by revolutionary methods can we succeed.) | Saunders Lewis, Tynged yr Iaith, BBC radio, 13 February 1962",
   "## Cymdeithas yr Iaith",
   "Wales listened. On 4 August 1962, at a summer school in Pontarddulais, Cymdeithas yr Iaith Gymraeg, the Welsh Language Society, was born. Its first protest came on 2 February 1963, when about seventy young people sat down on Trefechan Bridge in Aberystwyth and stopped the traffic for half an hour. A slate plaque on the bridge marks the spot today.",
   "!! bridge",
   "Over the years that followed, members of the Society painted out English-only road signs, pulled them down and dumped them at the doors of government. They refused to fill in forms and licences that were only in English, and they went to court and to prison rather than give way. More than a thousand people have stood in court for its campaigns. In 1970 the singer Dafydd Iwan was jailed for refusing to pay the fines for painting signs, and crowds gathered to demand his release. The campaign won: by the 1970s the government had agreed to bilingual road signs, and today the signs of Wales speak both languages.",
   "!! signs",
   "## 1967: ‘equal validity’",
   "In 1965 a committee under Sir David Hughes Parry reported that Welsh should have equal validity with English. The Welsh Language Act of 1967 gave Welsh that ‘equal validity’, let anyone use it in any court in Wales, and allowed official forms to be printed in Welsh. It also struck out the old law of 1746 that said the word ‘England’ in an Act of Parliament included Wales. Campaigners said it did not go far enough, and they kept up the pressure.",
   "## S4C: Gwynfor’s fast",
   "Radio Cymru began broadcasting in 1977. At the general election of 1979 both great parties promised a Welsh-language television channel. Within months the new government broke its promise. Members of Cymdeithas yr Iaith refused to pay their television licences, and some climbed transmitter masts. Then, in 1980, Gwynfor Evans, Plaid Cymru’s first Member of Parliament, announced that unless the promise was kept he would fast until death, from October. In September the government gave way. S4C went on air on 1 November 1982, the first television channel in the Welsh language.",
   "## 1993 and 2011: equal at last",
   "The Welsh Language Act of 1993 set down the principle that in public business and the courts in Wales, Welsh and English should be treated on a basis of equality. It set up a Welsh Language Board and made public bodies draw up schemes to serve people in Welsh. And it repealed what was left of Henry VIII’s Act, four hundred and fifty-seven years after it was passed.",
   "In 2010 the National Assembly for Wales passed the Welsh Language (Wales) Measure, which became law in 2011. Its first words are the words that every campaigner since Dan Isaac Davies had worked for:",
   "> The Welsh language has official status in Wales. | Welsh Language (Wales) Measure 2011, section 1",
   "The Measure created a Welsh Language Commissioner to see that public bodies keep their duties to Welsh speakers. In 2017 the Welsh Government set out its plan for a million Welsh speakers by 2050. The census of 2021 counted 538,300 people in Wales who speak Welsh.",
   "## What they could not take",
   "Look again at Henry VIII’s words: ‘utterly to extirp’. They tried for four hundred years, with Acts of Parliament, with reports, with inspectors and with a block of wood on a string. They failed. The language Saunders Lewis feared would be dead by now is heard on S4C and Radio Cymru, in the Senedd and the law courts, on road signs from Holyhead to Chepstow, in Welsh-medium schools in every county, and in the voices of children whose great-great-grandparents wore the Welsh Not.",
   "It survived because ordinary people would not let it go: the Bible readers of Griffith Jones’s schools, the men and women who answered the Blue Books, the teachers who brought Welsh back into the classroom, the Beasleys and their bailiffs, the young people on Trefechan Bridge, and the families who chose Welsh-medium schools for children who would never wear a Welsh Not. Welsh is the oldest language of this island still spoken every day. It is still here. Mae hen iaith yn fyw.",
  ],
